d23e ROUND 8 = 3 blocking, ALL ONE STRUCTURAL CLASS: a deliverable mandated in Scope or Testing with NO acceptance criterion covering it. B1 and B3 were the same point (AC4 said "a test asserts the deprecation warning is emitted" — SINGULAR — while Testing required two separately-exercised paths, so an implementer reading only the ACs writes one test and ships the exact round-6 dual-path gap); B2 was the `_deprecations.py:75-79` comment correction, mandated in BOLD in Scope with no criterion, so an executor could close every AC and ship a comment the code contradicts. Round 9 launched.

THIS CLASS HAD ALREADY APPEARED ONCE — round 4's example-configs finding was the same shape. So instead of patching the three findings I ran a SYSTEMATIC Scope+Testing -> AC coverage audit over the whole plan, which found FOUR MORE uncovered deliverables the reviewer had not flagged:
  * the `_scheduled(...)` REGISTRATION itself — no AC asserted the registry entry or its form, only that a warning is emitted, so the required form could have been silently wrong;
  * the CLI-precedence test;
  * the negative control (config-table `model` with the env var unset -> NO warning);
  * the neither-set control (unchanged defaults, no warning).
AC count 7 -> 12. Re-ran the audit after editing: 14 deliverables, ZERO gaps.

THE AUDIT ALSO FOUND A REAL CONTRADICTION the reviewer had not reached. Scope listed "the cutover runbook's kill-switch entry — restated in class terms" as a deliverable of THIS ticket, while the S8 section states this ticket does NOT redesign S8's recovery path AND `infra/runbooks/` is deliberately absent from file_impact. Three-way inconsistency. RESOLVED by removing it from Scope: it belongs to S8 (eb6e), which owns infra/runbooks/. This ticket's only obligation toward it is the durability note already recorded on eb6e for the operator. Verified afterwards that Scope no longer claims it and file_impact still excludes runbooks — consistent in both directions.

METHOD NOTE: the Scope/Testing -> AC coverage audit is now a standing pre-submit check for any plan, alongside the grep-every-mention consistency sweep. The two catch different classes — the sweep catches a stale statement contradicting a decision, the coverage audit catches a deliverable no criterion can prove was done. Round 8 had ZERO self-inflicted findings, the third consecutive round with none.
